{"step_index":1495,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:53:43Z","thinking":"**Directly analyzing the conjecture**\n\nThe `search_web` tool isn't functioning, but the problem can be addressed using existing reasoning and code abilities. Now, re-examining the conjecture: `∀ {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj], GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G → GraphCalc.HasRadius G → GraphCalc.radius G + 1 ≤ GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber G`.\n\n\n**Examining possible counterexamples**\n\nThe report mentions tests on graphs up to 6 vertices, implying radius is at most 5.  The radius of P₆ is 3. The conjecture `radius G + 1 ≤ romanDominationNumber G` appears to hold, given the tests and graph properties. However, a graph with radius ≥ 4 and n ≤ 6 seems impossible to construct.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python3 -c \\\"\\nimport networkx as nx\\n\\ndef roman_domination_number(G):\\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\\n    n = len(nodes)\\n    import itertools\\n    min_w = 2 * n\\n    for f in itertools.product([0, 1, 2], repeat=n):\\n        f_map = {nodes[i]: f[i] for i in range(n)}\\n        valid = True\\n        for v in nodes:\\n            if f_map[v] == 0:\\n                if not any(f_map[u] == 2 for u in G.neig